@@@!atrep-exo
@=at-tei=>tei

@@/ TEI P5 export for the at-tei syntax mapper. Genos variants
@@/ restore the camelCase element names; the flat fileDesc in
@@/ the header is schema-loose (strict titleStmt grouping is a
@@/ follow-up). Unknown genoses fall back to seg/ab with a type
@@/ attribute, so free genoses survive the trip.
@@/ The unseen attributes come from at-aphanes monosims held
@@/ inside the span, read through sim-name slots: who from
@@/ prosopon, ref from prosopon/chora/syllogos, target from
@@/ skopos, when from chronos, ana from eidos (one category per
@@/ monosim; a TEI ana with several pointers is a recorded
@@/ limitation of the slot join). A diaphane that holds a
@@/ paradosis renders as <choice> with the transmitted form as
@@/ <orig> and the reading as <reg> (a gate slot closes the
@@/ element only when the value is present); which of abbr, sic
@@/ or orig the source used is not kept. A diaphane opened by a
@@/ cite renders as a ref typed bibr around the printed
@@/ reference; a bare cite is a ptr typed bibr.

@%
& &amp;
< &lt;
> &gt;
" &quot;
%@

@@/ A document that holds a text block frames itself: the block
@@/ writes text and body, and the header comes from its lemma or
@@/ from the teiheader block before it.
@-> *document.text
<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<TEI xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0">
@(grammata)
</TEI>
>-@

@@/ A document with no text block is all body (a litogramma
@@/ document that opens with no title comes through the hom this
@@/ way): the frame TEI requires is written around it. The title
@@/ is left empty, which an importer reads as no title, so the
@@/ document comes back as it went out.
@-> *document
<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<TEI xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0">
<teiHeader>
<fileDesc>
<titleStmt>
<title/>
</titleStmt>
<publicationStmt>
@[@(!kolophon)
<availability>
]@<p>Exported from an atrep document.</p>
@[@(?!kolophon)</availability>
]@</publicationStmt>
<sourceDesc>
<p>An atrep document.</p>
</sourceDesc>
</fileDesc>
</teiHeader>
<text>
<body>
@(grammata)
</body>
</text>
</TEI>
>-@

@-> *paragraph
<p@[ ana="#@(eidos)"]@>@(grammata)</p>
>-@

@-> _.teiheader
<teiHeader>
<fileDesc>
@(grammata)
</fileDesc>
</teiHeader>
>-@

@@/ A text block that carries a lemma is a whole document come
@@/ through a morphism (the litogramma hom groups the document
@@/ under its title): the lemma is the title, and the header TEI
@@/ requires is written around it. A native text block has no
@@/ lemma and follows its own teiheader block.
@@/ The document's colophon (an embedded kolophon block, wherever
@@/ it stands) is drawn into the publication statement by its
@@/ dialektos slot, in both frames: its fields come first, as TEI
@@/ orders them, and the export note then stands as the
@@/ statement's availability, since a statement holds either
@@/ prose or fields. With no colophon the note is the statement.
@@/ A section cannot nest, so the header a lemma gates is written
@@/ as a run of sections, each gated on the lemma.
@-> _.text
@[<teiHeader>
<fileDesc>
<titleStmt>
<title>@(lemma)</title>
</titleStmt>
<publicationStmt>
]@@[@(?lemma)@(!kolophon)
<availability>
]@@[@(?lemma)<p>Exported from an atrep document.</p>
]@@[@(?lemma)@(?!kolophon)</availability>
]@@[@(?lemma)</publicationStmt>
<sourceDesc>
<p>An atrep document.</p>
</sourceDesc>
</fileDesc>
</teiHeader>
]@<text>
<body>
@(grammata)
</body>
</text>
>-@

@-> #
<div@[ xml:id="@(onym)"]@@[ type="@(genoses)"]@@[ n="@(taxis)"]@>
@[<head>@(lemma)</head>
]@@(grammata)
</div>
>-@

@-> #.front
<front>
@[<head>@(lemma)</head>
]@@(grammata)
</front>
>-@

@-> #.back
<back>
@[<head>@(lemma)</head>
]@@(grammata)
</back>
>-@

