@-> ,.emph
<emph>@(grammata)</emph>
>-@

@@/ Litogramma's emphasis arrives as `.emph`; when it also names
@@/ a TEI phrase element (the importer writes foreign, title and
@@/ term this way), the two-genos variants break the tie in
@@/ favour of the element named. A language genos beside foreign
@@/ is not written back as xml:lang.
@-> ,.emph.foreign
<foreign>@(grammata)</foreign>
>-@

@-> ,.emph.title
<title>@(grammata)</title>
>-@

@-> ,.emph.term
<term>@(grammata)</term>
>-@

@-> ,.hi
<hi>@(grammata)</hi>
>-@

@-> ,.foreign
<foreign>@(grammata)</foreign>
>-@

@-> ,.term
<term>@(grammata)</term>
>-@

@-> ,.mentioned
<mentioned>@(grammata)</mentioned>
>-@

@-> ,.socalled
<soCalled>@(grammata)</soCalled>
>-@

@-> ,.said
<said@[ who="#@(prosopon)"]@@[ ana="#@(eidos)"]@>@(grammata)</said>
>-@

@-> ,.said.indirect
<said direct="false"@[ who="#@(prosopon)"]@@[ ana="#@(eidos)"]@>@(grammata)</said>
>-@

@-> ,.said.thought
<said aloud="false"@[ who="#@(prosopon)"]@@[ ana="#@(eidos)"]@>@(grammata)</said>
>-@

@-> ,.said.indirect.thought
<said direct="false" aloud="false"@[ who="#@(prosopon)"]@@[ ana="#@(eidos)"]@>@(grammata)</said>
>-@

@@/ Litogramma's quotation arrives as `.q` with `.said` already
@@/ on it when it is speech; the two-genos variants break the tie
@@/ in favour of said.
@-> ,.said.q
<said@[ who="#@(prosopon)"]@@[ ana="#@(eidos)"]@>@(grammata)</said>
>-@

@-> ,.said.q.indirect
<said direct="false"@[ who="#@(prosopon)"]@@[ ana="#@(eidos)"]@>@(grammata)</said>
>-@

@-> ,.said.q.thought
